fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1072", description: some(value: "A DESCRIPTION BLOCK ON `entity` / `rule` / `constraint` / `fact` IS PARSED AND SILENTLY DROPPED — the same defect WI-1070 closed for `operation` and `const`, in the five remaining kinds §4.1 names.\n\nMEASURED 2026-08-10, driven — one declaration per source, one `{< blk >}` each, reading `anthill.reflect.DescriptionInfo` back through its own `target` field after a full stdlib load (the probe harness is `descriptions_of` in `rustland/anthill-core/tests/include/wi1070_operation_description_test.rs`):\n\n  entity Rec(n: Int64)            0 facts   -- DROPPED\n  entity W inside `sort Wrap`     0 facts   -- DROPPED\n  rule r: p(1)                    0 facts   -- DROPPED\n  constraint c :- p(?x), p(?x)    0 facts   -- DROPPED\n  fact q(2)                       0 facts   -- DROPPED\n  enum Col                        1 fact    -- WORKS (routes through `convert_sort_like`, which reads the field)\n  {< blk >} before a TOP-LEVEL `namespace`   PARSE ERROR: \"unexpected top-level node: description_block\"\n\nWHERE IT IS LOST. Same shape as WI-1070's: `grammar.js` gives every one of these productions a leading `repeat(field('description', $.description_block))`, and `parse::ir` has a `descriptions` field on exactly four types — `AbstractSort`, `SortWithBody`, and (WI-1070) `Operation`, `Const`. `Entity`, `Rule`, `Constraint`, `Fact`, `Namespace` have nowhere to put the text, so `convert_entity` / `convert_rule` / `convert_constraint` / `convert_fact` / `convert_namespace` never read the field. `enum` escapes only because `convert_item` routes `enum_declaration` through `convert_sort_like`, which already reads it.\n\nCORPUS COST IS NOT ZERO, unlike WI-1070's. Four stdlib sites lose their text TODAY, all in `stdlib/anthill/prelude/sort.anthill`: `entity Arrow` (311), `entity ExprCarried` (321), `entity RigidTypeProjection` (328), `entity Error` (348). These are substantive paragraphs — WI-791's arrow-arity note, WI-376's projection note, WI-428's rigid-receiver note — written by an author who had every reason to believe §4.1 preserved them.\n\nWHY THIS IS ONE TICKET AND NOT AN INLINE FOLLOW-ON TO WI-1070. Three of the five are mechanical repeats of WI-1070's fix (IR field + converter read + `emit_own_descriptions` on the declared symbol): `entity`, a LABELLED `rule`, a LABELLED `constraint`. Two are not, and need a decision before any code:\n\n  (a) WHAT IS THE TARGET of a description on an ANONYMOUS declaration? A `fact q(2)` has no name; an UNLABELLED `rule` / `constraint` has no citation handle either — 059 R3 already records that \"an unlabeled rule contributes nothing, correctly, having no citation handle for a `proof` or a `describe` to name it by\". Candidates: the head TERM (queryable, but a `DescriptionInfo` target that is not a name is a new shape for every reader of that fact), the clause's `RuleId`, or a REFUSAL (a description on an unnameable declaration is a load error — the \"loud over silent\" reading). Refusal is the option that costs nothing today: measured, no corpus site writes one.\n\n  (b) The TOP-LEVEL `namespace` case is a different mechanism — the block does not attach to `namespace_declaration` at file root at all; `convert_item` receives a bare `description_block` and raises \"unexpected top-level node\". It is at least LOUD, so it is the least urgent of the six, but §4.1 position 1 names `namespace` and a nested namespace should be checked separately from the top-level one.\n\nSCOPE: settle (a) first — that verdict is the ticket's real content. Then land the mechanical three plus whatever (a) decides for `fact` / unlabelled rule / unlabelled constraint. `emit_own_descriptions` (`kb/load.rs`) is the existing helper for a SYMBOL-targeted declaration and takes the mechanical three unchanged. Handle (b) or split it out with a reason, but say which.\n\nACCEPTANCE: for each kind admitted, an inline `{< … >}` emits a `DescriptionInfo` naming that declaration, DRIVEN by reading the fact back — not by asserting the program loads; the four stdlib `entity` sites above are shown to carry their text after the change (that is the corpus control, and it fails today); whatever (a) decides for an anonymous declaration is DRIVEN too, a refusal by pinning the diagnostic; a control shows `enum` and `sort` still emit exactly what they emit now, and `operation` / `const` (WI-1070) are unmoved. Each test says which case fails when the change is backed out. cargo-test green via rustland/scripts/test.sh.\n\nREFERENCE: WI-1070 (the same defect in `operation` / `const`; its test file is the harness and the precedent), kernel-language.md §4.1 (position 1 names `sort`, `operation`, `const`, `rule`, `entity`, `fact`, `constraint`, `namespace` — five of those are still false), `emit_own_descriptions` / `emit_desc_fact` in `kb/load.rs`, docs/proposals/059-secondary-entries.md (the unlabelled-rule-has-no-handle reading, for (a))."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-1070"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "codex", at: "2026-08-10T12:25:06Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1072", author: "user", content: "Implemented the named-target decision: descriptions on entities, labeled rules, labeled constraints, and namespaces emit anthill.reflect.DescriptionInfo against the qualified declaration symbol; descriptions on facts and unlabeled rules/constraints are refused in conversion with a pinned diagnostic because no stable citation target exists. Labeled constraints now receive Constraint symbols, namespace description blocks are consumed before item conversion, multi-head labeled rules emit metadata once, and the four existing stdlib entity paragraphs are recovered. Added driven WI-1072 tests covering direct/nested entities, multi-head and standalone labeled rules, labeled constraints, top-level/nested namespaces, anonymous refusals, all four stdlib sites, and sort/enum/operation/const controls. Red focused run: 1 control passed and 6 capability tests failed. Green focused run: 7/7; WI-1070 regression: 6/6; complete rustland/scripts/test.sh passed with 0 failures (log rustland/target/test-run-20260810-134743.log). Manual diff review found no issues. The required /code-review skill is not available in this session, so it could not be run; manual review was used and this limitation is recorded explicitly.", at: "2026-08-10T12:24:59Z")
